1
00:00:00,702 --> 00:00:03,397
This is a technical walkthrough of tracefork.

2
00:00:03,437 --> 00:00:06,868
First, what it does today, running locally on this machine.

3
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:10,561
Then, using mock UI, what it could become as a product.

4
00:00:10,963 --> 00:00:18,587
tracefork is a Python library that hooks one
place: the HTTP transport inside the Anthropic SDK.

5
00:00:18,627 --> 00:00:23,831
In record mode, every request and response
is copied, byte for byte, into a tape.

6
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:32,499
The tape is content-addressed. Payloads are stored as sha256
blobs, and an ordered event log chains them into one digest.

7
00:00:32,539 --> 00:00:37,865
Agents also read hidden inputs, like
the clock, UUIDs and random numbers.

8
00:00:37,905 --> 00:00:43,214
Those go through a NondetSource, which logs
every draw so it can be served back later.

9
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:47,235
In replay mode, the transport has no network behind it.

10
00:00:47,275 --> 00:00:53,858
Recorded responses are served back, and every request
the agent sends is hashed and checked against the tape.

11
00:00:53,898 --> 00:01:00,984
If every request matches, the replay is proven
identical. It costs nothing, and needs no API key.

12
00:01:01,024 --> 00:01:07,542
If anything drifts, replay fails loudly, and a
negative control checks that drift really is detected.

13
00:01:07,944 --> 00:01:11,000
Here is the original spike, run on this machine.

14
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:17,467
Two requests. Both hashes matched, the fingerprints
are identical, and there were zero network calls.

15
00:01:17,507 --> 00:01:23,618
The last check is the negative control: given
fresh nondeterminism, the drift was detected.

16
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:28,540
Forking builds on replay. You pick a
step, and swap in a different response.

17
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:32,647
The prefix before that step replays for free, and is still hash-checked.

18
00:01:32,687 --> 00:01:41,099
The swapped response is injected, and only the tail after it is
recorded fresh. That tail is the only part that can cost money.

19
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:48,708
Each branch gets a digest of its parent, its delta, and
the step it changed, so branches form a Merkle-style tree.

20
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:57,160
Blame is many forks. For each step, tracefork perturbs the
response k times, reruns the agent, and grades the outcome.

21
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:01,101
A step's flip rate is how often perturbing it changes the outcome.

22
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:05,901
Each rate gets a Wilson score interval,
because twenty trials is not certainty.

23
00:02:05,941 --> 00:02:10,077
Flip rate alone can't tell a root cause from a downstream echo of it.

24
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:15,081
So a temporal Shapley mode labels each
step as necessary, sufficient, or both.

25
00:02:15,483 --> 00:02:20,492
Now the UI. This is tracefork serve,
running locally against a seeded store.

26
00:02:20,532 --> 00:02:26,172
There's one run: a seven-exchange synthetic
agent, with faults planted at known steps.

27
00:02:26,575 --> 00:02:33,185
The timeline is on the left, the exact request and
response in the middle, and the analysis rail on the right.

28
00:02:33,225 --> 00:02:41,486
Blame ranks steps zero and one at a hundred percent
flip rate, with a 95 percent interval from 0.84 to 1.

29
00:02:41,526 --> 00:02:43,411
Every other step sits at zero.

30
00:02:43,451 --> 00:02:46,415
The badges on the timeline add the Shapley result.

31
00:02:46,455 --> 00:02:53,490
Step zero is necessary and sufficient. Step one is
only sufficient. It's an echo, not the root cause.

32
00:02:53,892 --> 00:02:58,908
Selecting an exchange shows the raw request
and response bytes that were recorded.

33
00:02:58,948 --> 00:03:04,773
The Forks tab shows the fork tree: three branches,
each with its divergence step and digest.

34
00:03:04,813 --> 00:03:07,990
And the Cost tab totals tokens and dollars by model.

35
00:03:08,391 --> 00:03:14,647
Forking from the UI is gated. The server only
forks agents that an operator has allowlisted.

36
00:03:14,687 --> 00:03:19,292
I'll fork the final exchange, and change
the answer from success to failure.

37
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:24,761
Forking the last step leaves an empty tail,
so the estimate is exactly zero dollars.

38
00:03:24,801 --> 00:03:28,602
Confirm, and the server runs a real fork and saves the branch.

39
00:03:28,642 --> 00:03:31,375
It shows up in the fork tree as a fourth branch.

40
00:03:31,778 --> 00:03:37,172
How do you know blame is right? tracefork tests
itself against faults with known root causes.

41
00:03:37,212 --> 00:03:45,360
validate plants five kinds of fault. Blame ranks the planted
step first every time, and the negative control stays at zero.

42
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:50,941
bench plants competing causes on one longer
tape. Ten of eleven resolve as planted.

43
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:57,376
The one miss is a documented limit of single-ordering
Shapley, and bench prints it, instead of hiding it.

44
00:03:57,778 --> 00:04:02,336
So that's today: a solid core, packaged as a local developer tool.

45
00:04:02,376 --> 00:04:07,978
It runs in one process, keeps tapes in
SQLite, and you instrument the agent yourself.

46
00:04:08,018 --> 00:04:15,431
There's no hosted ingest, no team view, and no
alerting. Evals exist only as internal self-validation.

47
00:04:15,471 --> 00:04:22,629
And bit-exact replay assumes a boundary: one process,
with time and randomness read through NondetSource.

48
00:04:23,031 --> 00:04:27,137
Now, what it could grow into: the same engine, inside a platform.

49
00:04:27,177 --> 00:04:32,637
An SDK captures runs at the same HTTP
seam, and ships them asynchronously.

50
00:04:32,637 --> 00:04:36,990
It fails open, and redacts secrets before anything leaves.

51
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:41,276
A thin ingestion API writes everything to a durable queue.

52
00:04:41,316 --> 00:04:48,420
Stream processors split each run into metadata, analytics
rows, and content-addressed tapes in object storage.

53
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:57,828
Next to that, a sandboxed worker pool runs evals, replays and forks
on demand. Most of that is free, because prefixes replay from tape.

54
00:04:58,231 --> 00:05:00,626
Everything from here on is mock UI.

55
00:05:00,666 --> 00:05:03,135
The entry point looks familiar: issues.

56
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:09,938
Failing runs are grouped by fingerprint: the failing step,
the tool, and the error class, instead of a stack trace.

57
00:05:09,978 --> 00:05:17,244
And failures aren't only exceptions. A low eval score, a
guardrail block, or a thumbs-down can open an issue too.

58
00:05:17,645 --> 00:05:24,739
Opening an issue shows a representative run as a span
waterfall, with tokens, latency and cost per step.

59
00:05:24,779 --> 00:05:29,550
Replay re-runs it from the tape, at zero
cost, and the hash check is visible.

60
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:36,737
Blame points to step three. The order lookup returned a
stale record, and the model reasoned correctly over bad data.

61
00:05:37,139 --> 00:05:42,949
Now test a fix without touching production:
fork step three, with a corrected tool result.

62
00:05:42,989 --> 00:05:46,710
The prefix replays for free, and only the tail re-executes.

63
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:51,330
The forked run ends with a correct refund message, and the eval passes.

64
00:05:51,731 --> 00:05:58,047
One click turns the failing run into an eval case:
the input, the expected behavior, and a scorer.

65
00:05:58,087 --> 00:06:03,674
Datasets built from real production failures
are the most useful test set a team can have.

66
00:06:03,714 --> 00:06:09,502
On every pull request that changes a prompt
or a model, the dataset runs as a CI gate.

67
00:06:09,542 --> 00:06:16,159
Scores come with confidence intervals, so a two-point
dip on forty cases isn't flagged as a regression.

68
00:06:16,562 --> 00:06:20,471
In production, a sample of live traffic is scored continuously.

69
00:06:20,511 --> 00:06:27,442
Cheap heuristics run on everything, an LLM judge
runs on a sample, and user feedback is folded in.

70
00:06:27,482 --> 00:06:35,115
When a provider silently updates a model, the pass rate drops
below the SLO, and the alert links to the affected runs.

71
00:06:35,517 --> 00:06:37,171
Put together, it's a loop.

72
00:06:37,211 --> 00:06:44,421
Capture runs, group failures, blame the step, curate the
case, gate on evals, fork to verify the fix, and ship.

73
00:06:44,461 --> 00:06:47,163
Every pass through the loop adds to the dataset.

74
00:06:47,563 --> 00:06:52,233
A sensible build order: a hosted
observe product first, to get installed.

75
00:06:52,273 --> 00:06:56,375
Then causal debugging in the UI, the part other tools don't have.

76
00:06:56,415 --> 00:07:03,127
Then evals, then the closed loop, and finally
enterprise: self-hosting, sandboxing and integrations.

77
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:09,815
Today, tracefork is the hard part: faithful,
hash-verified, zero-cost replay and forking.

78
00:07:09,855 --> 00:07:12,505
The product is the platform built around it.
